"| T10.1 | CAM logic, toolpaths, G-code | M | I | Toolpath Previewer |\n| T10.2 | Additive manufacturing constraints | M | I | Print Failure Predictor |\n| T10.3 | Failure analysis and material physics | M | S, C | Failure Cases; Virtual Load Test |\n\n## Level 4 Venture and release R1–R5\n\nFive modules and 52 units, all of the original's venture and release content with the duplication removed; every number-bearing unit is a simulator with a visible formula, and every legal unit is labelled literacy, not advice.\n\n### R1 The world as venture (12 units)\n\n| Unit | Concept | Depth | Field | Delivery | Interactive · media |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| R1.1 | One world, many carriers | O | VEN | I | Orbital Architecture Mapper |\n| R1.2 | Masterbrand positioning and non-negotiable codes | P | VEN, ALN | I, C | Brand Code Stress Test · img |\n| R1.3 | Orbital architecture: endorsed sub-brands, ventures, acquisitions | P | VEN | I | Orbital Mapper, rules mode |\n| R1.4 | Ecosystem orchestration across verticals | P | VEN | I | Vertical Flow Map |\n| R1.5 | Exclusivity and access | P | VEN | I | Access Tier Designer |\n",
"| R1.6 | Ritual and tribal signifiers | P | VEN, GRM.n | I | Ritual and Signifier Board |\n| R1.7 | Feedback loops and co-creation | P | VEN | I | Feedback Loop Architect |\n| R1.8 | Campaign logic: the campaign as world extension | P | VEN | I | Campaign-to-Premise Checker |\n| R1.9 | Bilingual and multi-script communication architecture | P | GRM.v | I | Script Pairing, campaign mode |\n| R1.10 | Activation mechanics: registration, tracking, journey | P | VEN | I | Activation Workflow Builder |\n| R1.11 | Full masterbrand and orbital architecture with rules of engagement | M | VEN | S, D | Orbital Mapper, full |\n| R1.12 | Campaign architecture | M | VEN | S, D | Campaign Builder |\n\n### R2 Money, law and rights (11 units)\n\n| Unit | Concept | Depth | Field | Delivery | Interactive · media |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| R2.1 | How long until you run out | O | RGT | I | Runway Simulator |\n| R2.2 | Bootstrapping versus institutional capital; dilution | P | RGT | L, I | Dilution Calculator |\n| R2.3 | Patronage, grants, public-private partnership | P | RGT | L, C | — |\n| R2.4 | Cash flow, burn, receivables against deposits | P | RGT | S | Runway Simulator, full |\n",
"| R2.5 | Intellectual property: marks, design rights, patents, copyright | P | RGT | I | IP Decision Tree |\n| R2.6 | Contracts, NDAs, transfer of rights | P | RGT | I | Contract Clause Explorer |\n| R2.7 | Cultural sovereignty and defensive protocols | P | RGT, SRC | L, I | Copy Response Playbook |\n| R2.8 | Attribution and consultation as protocol | P | SRC | I, D | template from F1.18 |\n| R2.9 | 18-month model with two scenarios | M | RGT | S, D | Venture Console (§9) |\n| R2.10 | IP and attribution protocol for the project | M | RGT | D | — |\n| R2.11 | Contract anatomy workshop (literacy) | M | RGT | L, I | Clause Explorer, full |\n\n### R3 Making it real (12 units)\n\n| Unit | Concept | Depth | Field | Delivery | Interactive · media |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| R3.1 | Who does what | O | HND | I | Collaboration Matrix |\n| R3.2 | The studio operating system: folders, versions, templates, agents | P | HND | I, T3 | Studio OS Kit |\n| R3.3 | Pitch architecture and defence | P | DOS | I, P | Pitch Builder; Client voice |\n| R3.4 | Resource management and specialist orchestration | P | HND | I | Specialist Vetting Scorecard |\n",
"| R3.5 | The collaboration matrix: dependencies, conflict, risk | P | HND | I | Collaboration Matrix, full |\n| R3.6 | Minimum viable implementation | P | HND | S, P | MVI Negotiator; Fabricator and CFO voices |\n| R3.7 | Release sequencing and phased drops | P | COL | I | Drop Sequencer |\n| R3.8 | The anatomy of the handover | P | HND | I | Handover Package Builder |\n| R3.9 | Governance, compliance, permissions | P | HND | I | Compliance Path Finder |\n| R3.10 | Briefing others: the brief as artifact | P | CMB | I, P | Brief Builder, full |\n| R3.11 | A complete handover package, audited | M | HND | S, D | Handover Auditor |\n| R3.12 | MVI negotiation at scale | M | HND | S, P | Venture Console with the DNA constants |\n\n### R4 Responsibility (8 units)\n\n| Unit | Concept | Depth | Field | Delivery | Interactive · media |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| R4.1 | The life after launch | O | LCY | I | Lifecycle Planner |\n| R4.2 | Sourcing, substitution, budget prioritisation | P | LCY, RGT | I | Substitution Trade-Off |\n| R4.3 | Packaging, distribution, interface | P | LCY | I | Unboxing and Distribution Storyboard |\n| R4.4 | Operation, maintenance, repair | P | LCY | I | Maintenance Budget |\n",
"| R4.5 | End-of-life architecture | P | LCY, PLW | I | End-of-Life Router |\n| R4.6 | The planetary ledger | P | PLW | S | Planetary Ledger, level 2 |\n| R4.7 | The final ledger with data | M | PLW | S, D | Planetary Ledger, level 3 |\n| R4.8 | Repair and end-of-life design for the studio project | M | LCY | S, D | area engine, lifecycle layer |\n\n### R5 Defence and dossier (9 units)\n\n| Unit | Concept | Depth | Field | Delivery | Interactive · media |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| R5.1 | A transfer brief in miniature | O | DOS | I | Transfer Brief Generator |\n| R5.2 | Systemic transfer: the method under an unfamiliar constraint | P | DOS | I | Transfer Brief Generator, full |\n| R5.3 | Critical defence: evidence, limits, the evolution of versions | P | DOS | L, P | all voices |\n| R5.4 | Dossier curation: alternatives, abandoned directions, history | P | DOS | I | Dossier Builder |\n| R5.5 | Specialist gap identification | P | DOS | I | Specialist Gap Finder |\n| R5.6 | Future-practice plan | P | DOS | I, D | Plan template |\n| R5.7 | The transfer brief, executed | M | DOS | S, K | the chosen engine under a new constraint |\n",
"| R5.8 | The defence room | M | DOS | S, P | Defence Room: three-voice panel |\n| R5.9 | The professional dossier | M | DOS | D | Dossier Builder, export |\n\n## Compound simulations\n\nSingle labs teach one concept; the program's central claim — that a world is a system in which form, body, material, money and consequence move together — can only be taught by simulations that couple several concepts, so ten engines are defined here, each built as one model with layers that units switch on one at a time.\n\n**The principle.** An engine holds one state (a site, an object, a garment, a DNA, a venture) and several coupled models. Early units switch on one layer and the engine behaves like a single lab. Later units switch on more, and the learner sees the couplings: change the wall thickness and the cost, weight, shipping volume, carbon and load safety all move; change a threshold in the DNA and the Stranger's outputs drift differently across media. Each engine's layers list is also its build order, so the first version is small.\n\n| Engine | State it holds | Layers (each added by a unit) | Units served | What it teaches that single labs cannot |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n",
"| 1. Timeline Atlas | The world set on a map and a timeline | Dominant and obscured strands; trade routes with object flows; object autopsy; credit map; regional layer; authoring for the learner's own lineage | F1 all; S3.14; S1.10; S5.11; S6.9 | That history is a network with flows, not a list; that any object sits at a crossing of routes, labour and meaning |\n| 2. Sense Engine | One scene with a body in it | Anthropometric fit; cognitive load; colour interaction with cultural overlays; light hierarchy; micro-interaction feedback; threshold sequence; proxemics | F3 all; F3.12 | That colour changes with light, that light changes what the body does, that sequence changes both |\n| 3. Stranger Lab | The learner's DNA | Text outputs; image outputs; 3D sketches; peer interpretation; drift scoring per grammar; rule editing and rerun; version comparison | M2.15, M2.18, M4.3, S5.21, T3.3 | That generativity is measurable; which rule is weak; how a fix in one grammar changes drift in another |\n",
"| 4. World Engine (space) | A site pack: plan, context, climate | Adjacency and zoning; circulation and sequence with a tension curve; daylight and motivated light; thermal mass and shading; acoustic character; back of house and egress; services coordination; CapEx, OpEx and yield; the learner's own drawings as input | S1.13–S1.33; F5; T4; T9; R3 | That a spatial decision is never only spatial: move a wall and the daylight, the staff path, the reverberation and the rent per square metre all move |\n| 5. Object Line | An object family from the Family Generator | Ergonomic fit; kinetic states; process route and tolerances; comfort stack for soft goods; load and fatigue; embedded components; BOM and value engineering with DNA flags; flat-pack and shipping; carbon and end of life; making constraints from fabrication | S2.13–S2.39; T2; T8; T9; T10; R4 | That the box, the factory and the recycling stream are design decisions made at the sketch |\n",
"| 6. Capsule | A body and a range | Ease and motion; grain, bias and drape; pattern and seams; grading across sizes; heat stress and microclimate; range plan with margins; tech-pack completeness; labour and material ledger | S3.16–S3.45; T4; T9 | That a fabric choice changes drape, yield, size grade, factory spec, cost and labour at once |\n| 7. Site Work (installation) | A public site and a duration | Scale against the body; light as mass; sound as boundary; audience rules and journey; decay or de-install schedule; live-data co-authorship; ephemeral debt; sponsor negotiation | S4.14–S4.36 | That awe, intimacy, complicity and ecological cost trade against one another in the same room |\n| 8. Identity Engine | The DNA's grammars | Mark; type system; colour system with contrast; tone of voice; grids and responsive layout; multi-script pairing; motion and sound; touchpoint stress test; guidelines tested by the Stranger | S5.13–S5.23; R1 | That an identity is the DNA made visible, and that one weak grammar shows up on every touchpoint |\n",
"| 9. Product Loop | A flow and a design system | Information architecture; states and errors; tokens and components from the DNA; copy and voice; accessibility audit; motion; handover spec | S6.10–S6.19 | That accessibility, content and tokens are one system, not three checks |\n| 10. Venture Console | The world as a business | Runway and burn; dilution scenarios; margin stacks per carrier; IP routes and costs; MVI trade-offs against DNA constants; orbital architecture; handover readiness; the planetary ledger | R1–R4; S1.29; S2.34; S3.32 | That cutting cost or time breaks named constants, and that a funding route has a price in control |\n\n**Cross-engine couplings worth building.** Object Line to Venture Console (BOM into margin); World Engine to Venture Console (fit-out into runway); every engine to the Planetary Ledger (materials and energy into carbon); every engine to the Stranger Lab (outputs judged against the DNA); every engine to the Handover Auditor (exports checked for what a maker would misread).\n\n",
"**Build staging.** Each engine starts with two coupled layers and a visible formula panel, adds one layer per unit as the content is written, and is never built past the units that exist. The Stranger Lab, Sense Engine and Timeline Atlas come first because Foundations and Method need them; World Engine and Object Line next because they prove the studios; the rest follow their areas.\n\n## Media assets study\n\nAbout 70% of the units need media beyond text, and nearly all of it can be sourced free or produced with the tools in this account; the expensive kind of asset (commissioned photography, filmed practitioners) is replaced by open collections, public talks and drawings we make ourselves.\n\n| Asset type | Units that need it | Volume | Free source | Produce ourselves |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Object and artwork images (img) | Every C unit; F1 world sets; S2, S3, S5 cases | 40–60 per module, about 1,200 total | CC0 museum collections and their APIs; Wikimedia; public-domain surveys | none needed; licence note per image |\n",
"| Drawings, plans, sections (drw) | F2, S1, S2.24, S3.23, T1 | 150–250 | Archnet; public-domain survey drawings; Wikimedia; OSM for plans | SVG drawings redrawn to one house style, in-app |\n| 3D models (3d) | F2.1, F3.8, S1, S2, S4, T4, T5 | 60–100 | Smithsonian 3D; CC models on Sketchfab; Scan the World | Blender models for archetypes and site packs |\n| Short video (vid) | F1.13, F3.5, S6.9, T1.3, T6.3; anything where motion carries meaning (drape, mechanisms, light changing) | 40–60 clips, under 8 minutes | embedded public talks and museum channels | screen recordings of the engines; short AI-narrated explainers only where text fails |\n| Animated diagrams (ani) | F1.1, F5.3, O1.3, M2.6, and any process explained | 80–120 | — | SVG and JS animations in-app; Figma for stills |\n| Audio (aud) | F3.9, S1.22, S2.14, S4.24–26, T4.5, S5.18 | 60–100 clips | CC field recordings; the learner's own | synthesised feedback sounds; spatialised demos |\n| Data (dat) | F1.11, F5, S1.19, S1.24, T7, T9, R2 | 20–30 sets | Our World in Data; World Bank; UN; climate services; OSM | cleaned subsets with attribution |\n| Fonts | F4.8–9, S5, R1.9 | 30–50 families | Google Fonts | none |\n",
"| Site, object, body, brand packs (pack) | S1.33, S2.39, S3.45, S4.36, S5.23, S6.19 and regional slots | 2–3 per area | OSM plans; CC0 buildings; public scans; public-domain identities; well-known flows | assembled and documented per area |\n| Case dossiers | Every C unit | about 300 | the facts from open sources | one page each: story, image, licence, which units use it |\n| Partner prompts | Every P unit; the five voices | 60–80 prompt architectures | — | written, tested, versioned |\n\n**Rules.** A unit's media list is part of its spec; no unit is Drafted without its media sourced or scheduled. Images are referenced by link where licence requires and stored where CC0 allows. Every asset carries its licence, source and the units that use it, so a takedown or a swap touches one record.\n\n## Lecture material study\n\nLecture material is whatever must be told rather than discovered — sequenced history, argument, definitions, procedures, warnings — and the program keeps it to about a fifth of learner time by converting each kind into the form that lets the learner meet it through doing first.\n\n**What counts as lecture material**\n\n| Kind | Examples in this program | Why it must be told |\n",
"|---|---|---|\n| Sequenced narrative | F1's pivots and centres; S3.11 history of silhouette; S5.11 identity histories | Order and causation are the content |\n| Theory and argument | S1.9 phenomenology; S4.12 relational theory; F2.4 typology theory; S2.10 critical design | The claim and the counter-claim are the content |\n| Definitions and taxonomies | M2.6 the twelve grammars; M3.3 roles; R2.5 IP types | Shared vocabulary is needed before practice |\n| Procedures | M1.3 research routes; R3.8 handover anatomy; M1.5 ethics and consent | Steps, in order, with the reasons |\n| Cases | Every C unit | The specific is what makes the general stick |\n| Warnings and ethics | F1.18 attribution; T3.5 bias; R2.7 sovereignty | Consequences must be named |\n\n**How each kind converts**\n\n| Kind | Conversion | What remains as reading or video |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sequenced narrative | Told through the Timeline Atlas and object stories; the learner navigates before reading | One 400-word card per pivot or centre: why it mattered, what to look for |\n",
"| Theory and argument | An argument card (claim, counter-claim, one case) followed by the lab that makes the theory felt: phenomenology through the Light Sandbox, relational theory through the Audience Ritual Builder | Short original essays, because the canonical texts are not free and a summary that replaces them is not allowed |\n| Definitions and taxonomies | Sort first, then reveal: the learner sorts examples into bins, the taxonomy is named after | A glossary entry and a one-line \"say it to a designer\" |\n| Procedures | Embedded as the interactive's steps; the tool is the procedure | A checklist to keep |\n| Cases | World-set dossiers tagged to units; never lectured | The dossier page itself |\n| Warnings and ethics | Decision simulators: the Spectrum Sort, the Sponsor Negotiation, the MVI Negotiator | A principle stated once, in a sentence |\n\n**Format rules for what remains.** Text first, with optional narrated audio; video only where motion carries meaning; nothing over eight minutes; every lecture element names the lab that precedes it and the artifact field it feeds; the original's voice is allowed in provocations and studio briefs, nowhere else.\n\n",
"**Share of time, estimated across the comprehensive version.** Lecture material about 20%; cases about 15%; single labs about 25%; compound simulations about 20%; making and documents about 15%; the partner about 5%. Foundations is heavier in lecture and cases; Method and the studios are heavier in labs and engines.\n\n## Research log and evolution rules\n\nResearch is uncapped; what is capped is losing it, so every module carries a research log, every finding that changes the structure is recorded as a version, and the structure itself expects to be wrong in places.\n\n**The research log per module** (one living document each): questions open; sources found, with licence and quality grade; world-set candidates and the units they would serve; claims sheet (every factual statement in unit copy with its source); findings that argue for a structural change; open questions for you. Research sprints are dated entries in the log and can be re-run at any time.\n\n**How a finding changes the structure**\n\n| Finding | Structural move | Version effect |\n|---|---|---|\n| A concept the units do not cover | Add a unit, at the depth the concept needs | minor (v2.x) |\n",
"| A unit that is really two | Split | minor |\n| Two units that are one | Merge; keep both numbers as aliases | minor |\n| A concept sitting at the wrong depth | Re-depth (O to P, P to M, or back) | minor; slices update automatically |\n| A module that belongs elsewhere | Move; update the artifact fields it feeds | major (v3.0) |\n| A better engine coupling | Add a layer to an engine; note the unit that switches it on | minor |\n| A media asset that cannot be sourced free | Swap or produce; record in the asset register | none |\n\n**Change log** (append here)\n\n- v2.1 — Pass 1 master structure created from Concept v2; 21 modules, 10 tools modules, 10 engines, about 430 units.\n\n- v3.0 — Audit against all earlier versions (see Final Structure v3.0): 22 units added, 2 renamed, six threads, closing tests, prerequisites, seven partner voices, commissioning variants of every studio brief. Unit count about 450. The additions register below is appended here so this document stays the single unit register.\n\n**Additions register, v3.0** (columns as the unit tables; Follows = the unit each new one sits after)\n\n| Unit | Concept | Depth | Field | Delivery | Interactive · media | Follows |\n",
"|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| F1.3a | Interdisciplinary discourse: what film, performance, sound, gaming and digital culture teach design | P | SRC, GRM.n | L, C | Cross-Discipline Case Wall · vid, aud, img | F1.3 |\n| F1.20a | Vernacular intelligence: oral history, folktale and local taxonomy as sources, with consent | M | SRC | L, K, D | Oral History Kit · aud | F1.20 |\n| F4.7 | Renamed: Semiotics, symbolism and collective memory | P | GRM.n | L, C | Sign-Swap · img | — |\n| F5.5a | Land, water and climate as the site | P | PER, PLW | L, C, I | Site Systems Map · dat | F5.5 |\n| M1.11a | World-building lessons from film, games and performance | P | GRM.n, GRM.t | L, C | Bible Anatomy · vid | M1.11 |\n| S1.1a, S2.1a, S3.1a, S4.1a, S5.1a, S6.1a | The practice DNA of this medium: what it adds and forbids | P | ALN | L, I | Practice DNA card | Sx.1 |\n| S1.8a | Exhibition, museum and cultural-district experience | P | GRM.s, GRM.n | C, I | Visitor Journey Mapper · img, drw | S1.8 |\n| S1.8b | The service script behind the space: hospitality, food and drink, operations | P | GRM.b, HND | C, I | Service Blueprint Builder | S1.8a |\n",
"| S1.24a | The outdoor room: landscape, shade, water, ground | M | GRM.s, GRM.a | C, S | World Engine, outdoor layer · dat | S1.24 |\n| S3.35a | Accessories and jewellery: the object on the body | M | GRM.f, GRM.k | C, K, I | Fit Tester, body mode · img | S3.35 |\n| T4.2a | Data-rich models: BIM and PLM | M | HND | L, I | Data-Rich Model Explorer · 3d | T4.2 |\n| T5.1a | Documenting physical work: photography, capture, sequencing | P | DOS | L, K, I | Documentation Planner · img | T5.1 |\n| R1.10a | The physics of the checkout | P | VEN | C, I | Checkout Flow Dissector | R1.10 |\n| R1.10b | Online-to-offline synchronisation | P | VEN | I | O2O Journey Mapper | R1.10a |\n| R1.10c | The flagship as media | P | VEN | C, I | Media-Value Calculator · img | R1.10b |\n| R1.12a | Large-scale activation: expos, festivals, civic events | M | VEN | C, S | Activation Workflow, scale mode · img | R1.12 |\n| R2.4a | Cost, value and margin architecture | P | RGT | L, S | Venture Console, margin layer | R2.4 |\n| R2.4b | Expansion across markets | P | RGT | L, I | Expansion Canvas | R2.4a |\n| R2.6 | Renamed: Contracts, NDAs, licensing, transfer of rights | P | RGT | I | Contract Clause Explorer | — |\n",
"| R3.6a | Client and stakeholder negotiation | P | HND, CMB | S, P | Negotiation Simulator; Client voice | R3.6 |\n| R4.4a | Lifecycle economics: total cost of ownership | P | LCY, RGT | S | Venture Console, lifecycle layer | R4.4 |\n\n**Rule changes.** Every Sx studio brief has Make (ANC) and Commission (CMB) variants; T4 retitled Simulation, interface and data-rich models; T5 retitled Honest evidence and documentation; S1.21 gains scent and culinary case dossiers.\n\n- v3.1 and v3.2 — Final Structure revisions with no unit changes: presentation forms and the consumption matrix (v3.1); no fixed unit sequence, ten opening moves and four invariants (v3.2).\n- v3.3 — F1 redesigned in Pass 2: 23 units (21 plus two v3.0 additions) become 31; the pivots become recurrences, the continents become worlds joined by networks, eight units added; F1 renumbered with a Was mapping.\n- v3.4 — F1 sprints 2 and 3: F1.19a War, crisis and making, F1.22a Use and consumption and F1.30a Counterfactual history added; 34 units.\n",
"- v3.5 — F1 sprint 4: technique becomes the autopsy's fifth layer; F1.13 carries objects, materials and skills; heritage-brand and restitution cases in F1.24 and F1.17; the Lineage view opens at F1.1.\n- v3.6 — F1 sprints 5 and 6: F1.28a Reconstruction added (its voice and forms to confirm in its spec); each world chapter ends on a living practice and has a named device; Practice Reading; the human-flow rule in F1.13; the state-authored heritage list in F1.3; 35 units. Merged into this register on 2026-09-28 after gate G1. Program unit count about 460.\n\n**Research status by module.** All modules Open. Sprint order proposed: F1, M2, S2 (the two proofs plus the first studio), then the rest of Foundations and Method, then areas, then venture.\n\n## Pass 2\n\nPass 2 takes one module through the whole workflow with research uncapped, and its first output is a research log, not a lesson.\n\n**Workflow for a module** (the Module Factory)\n\n1. Research sprint: open questions, sources, world-set candidates, claims sheet, structural findings.\n2. Structure update: any adds, splits, merges or re-depths, versioned in §12.\n",
"3. Unit specs: for each unit, the essential question, the lab, the lecture element and its conversion, the cases, the Apply for both lanes, the partner voice, the artifact field, the media list.\n4. Engine layers: which layers this module switches on, with the formulas and couplings.\n5. Copy: unit text in the program voice; argument cards; glossary entries.\n6. Prototypes: the module's labs as working pages; engine layers added.\n7. Partner prompts: written and tested against the module's units.\n8. Checks: accessibility, claims against sources, licences.\n9. Your review; revisions; status to Drafted, then Tested.\n\n**Proposed first module: F1 Histories of making.** It exercises the world set, the Timeline Atlas engine, the free-collection sourcing, the lecture-to-lab conversion for narrative history, and the reference-to-appropriation ethics that the whole program depends on. M2 follows as the second proof.\n\n**What I need from you before Pass 2 starts:** confirmation of F1 (or another module), and any objections to the depths and unit lists above — comment on the rows themselves.\n"
